Q: What is the difference between link wrapping and &#39;use client&#39; in Next.js? A: <p>It’s been a minute since I’ve used Next, so if someone chimes in here or documents exist that contradict what I say then trust those other sources more. I’m also not familiar with that button component you’re using from what appears to be the <code>mantine</code> package, so depending on how they implement it, that could also make a difference.</p>
<p>But the <code>'use client'</code> directive and <code>Link</code> component are different things and serve different purposes. If you're creating a component that needs client side features, such as <code>localstorage</code>, then it needs to be a client component (and needs the <code>'use client'</code> directive). Generally, if something doesn't have to be a client component then it shouldn't be (as @chhorm-ratha mentioned).</p>
<p>The <code>Link</code> component is for route navigation <em>within your NextJS web app</em>. Note that this isn't the same as an <code>a</code> (anchor) component which <em>can</em> be used for route navigation within your NextJS app, however it can also be used to navigate to web pages outside of your web app as well.</p>
<p><code>'use server'</code> really has little to no bearing on the use of <code>Link</code> and vice versa.</p>
<p>In your first code snippet example</p>
<pre class="lang-jsx prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Link href=&quot;/voice_monitor&quot; passHref legacyBehavior&gt;
  &lt;Button component=&quot;a&quot;&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/Button&gt;
&lt;/Link&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>You're wrapping a <code>Button</code> in a <code>Link</code> component and then setting the <code>component</code> prop of the <code>Button</code> component nested within the <code>Link</code> component to an <code>a</code> (anchor) element. Generally, most libraries that implement a <code>component</code> prop of a component makes it so that component actually gets rendered in the browser as the component passed to the <code>component</code> prop. So if you were to do something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-jsx prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Button component=&quot;a&quot;&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/Button&gt;
&lt;Button component={Link} href=&quot;/voice_monitor&quot;&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/Button&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The actual rendered html for that Button component would be:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>// note the missing href attribute since you didn't set it in the button component
&lt;a&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;Link href=&quot;/voice_monitor&quot;&gt;Go to Voice Monitor&lt;/Link&gt;
// technically, I believe Link components actually get rendered as anchor elements so the line directly above this isn't actually 100% correct to what gets rendered in the browser
</code></pre>
<p>So you're essentially wrapping an <em>anchor</em> element in a <code>Link</code> component which doesn't really make sense and is probably not what you're intending or wanting to do as their functionality overlaps.</p>

Q: Get decimal portion of a number with JavaScript A: <p>Try this:</p>
<pre><code>var _num = 3.1415926535;
var _decs = `${_num}`.includes( &quot;.&quot; ) ? ( ( `${_num}`.split( /\./g ) )[1] ) : &quot;0&quot;;
var _out = `0.${_decs}`;
console.log( _num, _decs, _out );
</code></pre>
